      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the total surface area of the pyramid, we can break the problem into two parts: finding the area of the base and finding the area of the four triangular faces.\n\n1. The base\nThe base of the pyramid is a square with side length 10 cm, so the area of the base is \\(10 \\times 10 = 100\\) square centimeters. (I multiplied the length of a side of the square by itself to get the area.)\n\n2. The area of the triangular faces\nThe base of each triangular face is the side length of the square, so it is 10 cm. The height of each triangular face can be found using the Pythagorean theorem, since the peak of the pyramid is 12 cm above the center of the base (and half a diagonal of the base). The half-diagonal of the square is \\(10 \\ \\text{cm} / 2 = 5 \\ \\text{cm}\\), so the height of the triangular face is \\(\\sqrt{12^2 - 5^2} = 11 \\ \\text{cm}\\). The area of each triangular face is \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) base times height, so it is \\(\\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 11 = 55\\) square centimeters. Since there are four triangular faces, the total area of the triangular faces is \\(4 \\times 55 = 220\\) square centimeters.\n\n3. The total surface area\nThe total surface area of the pyramid is the sum of the area of the base and the area of the four triangular faces, so it is \\(100 + 220 = \\boxed{320}\\) square centimeters.",
