    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Step 1: Find the slant height of the pyramid.\nTo find the slant height, we can use the Pythagorean theorem. We can imagine a right triangular prism inside the pyramid. The height of this prism is half the side length of the base, which is 5 cm. The plane of the prism is parallel to the base of the pyramid. The slant height of the pyramid is the same as the height of the triangular base of the prism.\n[Pythagorean theorem - h^2 = (12 cm)^2 + 5^2]\nh = √(144 + 25) cm = √169 cm ≈ 13 cm\n\nStep 2: Find the area of the four triangular sides of the pyramid.\nWe can divide the pyramid into two similar isosceles triangles, each with a base of 10 cm and a height of 13 cm. The area of one triangle is (1/2) * 10 * 13 = 65 cm^2. Since there are two triangles, the total area of the four triangular sides is 2 * 65 cm^2 = 130 cm^2.\n\nStep 3: Find the area of the square base of the pyramid.\nThe area of the square base is 10^2 = 100 cm^2.\n\nStep 4: Find the total surface area of the pyramid.\nThe total surface area of the pyramid is the sum of the area of the four triangular sides and the area of the square base: 130 cm^2 + 100 cm^2 = 230 cm^2.\nThe total surface area of the pyramid is 230 square centimeters.\nThe answer is 230.",
